    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Accomodation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A boarding house or hostel is a type of temporary lodging provided to a person as a guest or for a fee in a place that will serve them at least overnight.
People traveling away from home for more than a day need accommodations to sleep in, but also to ensure safety, protection from the cold or bad weather, storage for their luggage, and access to common bathroom facilities.
The most common form of commercial lodging is a hotel, but it can also be a motel, hostel, inn, or commercial accommodations in a private home for a fee, such as a bed and breakfast, guesthouse, or vacation rental.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Accomodation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lodging</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Accomodation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Lodging</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Andalucia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Andalusia (Andalucia in Spanish) is an autonomous community of Spain recognized as a historical nationality by its Statute of Autonomy. It is made up of eight provinces: Almería, Cádiz, Córdoba, Granada, Huelva, Jaén, Málaga and Seville. Its capital and most populous city is Seville, seat of the Governing Council of the Andalusian Regional Government, the Parliament and the Presidency of the Regional Government of Andalusia.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Andalucia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andalusia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Andalucia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Andalusia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Andalucia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Andalusia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Andalusian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The autonomous community of Andalusia has a rich, varied cuisine, with variations between the coast and the interior. It is part of the Mediterranean diet. Its cuisine is closely linked to the use of olive oil, nuts, fish, and meat. Pastries are strongly influenced by Andalusian cuisine, with the use of almonds and honey. The region&apos;s Christmas sweets, such as mantecados, polvorones, and alfajores, are well-known.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Andalusian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andalusian_cuisine</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Andalusian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Andalusian_cuisine</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Andalusian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Andalusian_cuisine</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Aragón</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Aragon (Aragón in Spanish) is a Spanish autonomous community, resulting from the historic kingdom of the same name. It encompasses the central stretch of the Ebro Valley, the central Pyrenees, and part of the Iberian Peninsula in the north of the country. It is defined in its statute of autonomy as a historical nationality.
The Kingdom of Aragon, along with the Principality of Catalonia, the kingdoms of Valencia and Majorca, and other territories of France, Italy, and Greece, formed the historic Crown of Aragon for centuries. The current autonomous community was established in 1982, comprising the provinces of Huesca, Teruel, and Zaragoza. Its capital is the city of Zaragoza.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Aragón</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aragon</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Aragón</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Aragon</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Atraction</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A tourist attraction is a place of interest that tourists visit, typically for its inherent or exhibited cultural value, historical significance, beauty, and leisure, adventure, and fun.
Attractions are crucial elements in motivating tourists to travel, and the influx of tourists to a location generally generates both related economic activity (hotels, restaurants, tour agencies, local businesses, among others) and infrastructure development for access to and enjoyment of the tourist attraction (airports, routes, signage, etc.).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Atraction</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tourist_attraction</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Atraction</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Attraction</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Barcelona is a Spanish municipality and city, the capital of Catalonia, the province of Barcelona, ​​and the Barcelonés region. With a population of 1,702,814 as of January 2024, it is the second most populous city in Spain and the Iberian Peninsula after Madrid, and the tenth in the European Union. Its metropolitan area has a population of 5,797,356 according to Eurostat, making it the fifth most populous urban agglomeration and the most densely populated in the European Union.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
