    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Castell_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The only castle in Barcelona is the Montjuïch Castle.
Montjuïc Castle (Catalan: Castell de Montjuïc) is a former military fortress located on Montjuïc Mountain, in the city of Barcelona. The fortress was once a Spanish Army installation, although it was later transferred to the city council, which currently manages it. Historically, the castle has played an important role in various episodes of Barcelona&apos;s history.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Castell_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castell_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Castilla_La_Mancha</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castilla-La Mancha is an autonomous community of Spain, according to its Statute of Autonomy and the Spanish Constitution. Its distinctive name indicates that this region is &quot;the part of Castile around the region of La Mancha,&quot; establishing a similarity with the traditional name of Castilla la Nueva (a predecessor region that also included the province of Madrid). It is made up of the provinces of Albacete, Ciudad Real, Cuenca, Guadalajara, and Toledo, which are in turn divided into a total of 919 municipalities. Its highest executive and legislative body is the Regional Government of Castilla-La Mancha, headquartered in Toledo; and its most populous city, Albacete, is the seat of the highest judicial body (the High Court of Justice of Castilla-La Mancha).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Castilla_La_Mancha</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castilla_La_Mancha</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">There is no castle within the city of Bilbao, the closest one is Butrón Castle.
Butrón Castle takes us back to the Middle Ages, to the time of the factional wars between different factions. It is located in Gatika, halfway between Bilbao and San Juan de Gaztelugatxe, on a hill atop some rocks, surrounded by a centuries-old forest.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castillo_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">There is no castle in the city of Valencia, but it was a walled city, with two imposing gates at the entrance to the fortress: the Quart Tower and the Serranos Tower.
The Quart Towers or Quart Gate were one of the two fortified gates of Valencia&apos;s medieval walls that still stand. The complex consists of two semi-cylindrical towers joined by a central body, where the gate itself opens, shaped like a semicircular arch. They owe their name to the fact that they were located on the road that led from the city center, from Plaza de La Virgen, where Valencia Cathedral is located, to the village of Cuart de Poblet. The towers were the western access for traffic from Castile.
The Serrano Towers or Serranos Gate are made up of two polygonal towers joined by a central body, where the gate itself opens, topped with a semicircular arch.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castillo_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Aljafería Palace is a fortified palace built in Zaragoza in the second half of the 11th century at the initiative of al-Muqtadir as the residence of the Hudid kings of Saraqusta. This recreational palace (then called Qasr al-Surur or &apos;Palace of Joy&apos;) reflects the splendor achieved by the Taifa kingdom during its period of political and cultural apogee.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Castillo_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Castillo_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catalunya</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catalonia (Catalan: Catalunya; Aranese: Catalonha; Spanish: Cataluña) is an autonomous community of Spain, considered a historical nationality in its Statute of Autonomy. Located in the northeast of the Iberian Peninsula, it occupies a territory of approximately 32,000 km².</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Catalunya</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catalonia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Catalunya</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catalonia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Metropolitan Cathedral Basilica of the Holy Cross and Saint Eulalia (Catalan: Catedral de la Santa Creu i Santa Eulàlia)—also known as the Cathedral—(Catalan: Seu) is the Gothic cathedral of Barcelona, ​​the seat of the Archdiocese of Barcelona, ​​in Catalonia, Spain.
The current cathedral was built during the 13th to 15th centuries on the site of the former Romanesque cathedral, itself built on a Visigothic church preceded by an early Christian basilica, the remains of which can be seen underground in the Museum of the History of the City. The completion of the imposing façade in the same style, however, is much more modern (19th century).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catedral_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Cathedral Basilica of Santiago (Basque: Done Jakue Katedrala) is the Catholic church that has housed the seat of the Diocese of Bilbao in Bilbao (Vizcaya, Basque Country, Spain) since 1949. It was built between the last quarter of the 14th century and the beginning of the 16th century in the Gothic style, although its façade and tower are the result of a thorough reconstruction undertaken in the 19th century in the Neo-Gothic style. The cathedral takes its name from the patron saint of Bilbao, the apostle Saint James the Greater, in relation to the passage through the city of a coastal branch of the Camino de Santiago. It is the most monumental Gothic church in Vizcaya and also serves as a parish church.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catedral_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Metropolitan Cathedral-Basilica of the Assumption of Our Lady of Valencia, popularly known as La Seu in Valencian, is the seat of the Archbishopric of Valencia and is dedicated, at the request of James I—following the 13th-century tradition—to the Assumption of Mary. It was consecrated in 1238 by the first bishop of Valencia after the Reconquista, Friar Andrés de Albalat.
Valencian Gothic is the predominant construction style of this cathedral, although it also contains elements of Romanesque, French Gothic, Renaissance, Baroque, and Neoclassical styles.
Inside, the Holy Chalice, dating from the 1st century and given to the cathedral by King Alfonso the Magnanimous in 1437, is venerated.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catedral_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Catedral_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Catedral_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Seo del Salvador en su Epifanía is one of the two metropolitan sees or cathedrals of the archdiocese of Zaragoza, along with the cathedral-basilica of Our Lady of the Pillar. It is located in the city of Zaragoza (Aragon, Spain). In the diocese it is usually called simply the Seo, as it was the first cathedral as opposed to the also minor basilica, &quot;el Pilar&quot;. It was built on the site of the ancient Roman forum of Caesaraugusta and the great mosque of Saraqusta, whose minaret still bears a mark on the current tower. The building was begun in the 12th century in the Romanesque style, integrated into the main mosque, and has been the subject of many renovations and extensions until 1704, the year in which the Baroque spire was placed on top of the tower. Although it was not until 1788 when the 4 statues representing the cardinal virtues were placed in the new tower of the Seo, above the body of the clock tower.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#City-</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A city is a human settlement of considerable size. The term &quot;city&quot; has different meanings around the world, and in some places the settlement may be very small. Even when the term is limited to larger settlements, there is no universally accepted definition of the lower limit of its size. In a narrower sense, a city can be defined as a permanent, densely populated place with defined administrative boundaries, whose inhabitants work primarily in non-agricultural activities.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#City-</IRI>
