        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Coordinates</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Geographic coordinates are a reference system that allows each location on Earth to be specified by a set of numbers, letters, or symbols. The coordinates are generally chosen so that two references represent the horizontal position, and a third represents the altitude. The horizontal position coordinates used are latitude and longitude, a spherical or spheroidal angular coordinate system centered on the Earth and usually expressed in sexagesimal degrees..</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Coordinates</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geographic_coordinate_system</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Coordinates</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Coordinates</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Coordinates</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Geographic_coordinate_system</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Country</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A country is a territory with its own geographical and cultural characteristics, which may or may not constitute a sovereign state or a political entity within a state.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Country</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Country</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Country</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Country</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Country</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Country</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#CuisineType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A set of dishes and culinary customs specific to a particular location.
In this ontology, we differentiate, in a first approximation, between Japanese, Andalusian, Mexican, Italian, and Mediterranean cuisine.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#CuisineType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">CuisineType</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Cultural</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A cultural event is an organized activity to promote and share the artistic expressions, knowledge, traditions, values, or customs of a community. These events can range from art exhibitions to music festivals, religious celebrations, or activities that convey historical knowledge. In essence, cultural events are platforms for expression, learning, and the celebration of cultural identity.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Cultural</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Cultural</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#CulturalHeritage</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Cultural heritage is the cultural inheritance of a community&apos;s past, maintained to the present day and passed on to future generations.
Entities that identify and classify certain assets as relevant to the culture of a people, a region, or all of humanity also ensure the safeguarding and protection of these assets, so that they are duly preserved for future generations and can be studied and a source of emotional experiences for all those who use, enjoy, or visit them.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#CulturalHeritage</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_heritage</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#CulturalHeritage</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">CulturalHeritage</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Destination</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A destination is a country, state, region, city, or town which is marketed or markets itself as a place for tourists to visit. It may contain one or more tourist attractions.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Destination</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Destination</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Euzkadi</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Basque Country or Euskadi (in Basque: Euskal Herria or Euskadi) is a Spanish autonomous community, considered a historical nationality, located on the eastern edge of the Cantabrian Sea coast, bordering France. It consists of the provinces and historical territories of Álava, Guipúzcoa, and Vizcaya. The most populous city is Bilbao, followed by Vitoria and San Sebastián. Its population is 2,227,684; about half live in the Bilbao metropolitan area. It borders Navarre and the French department of Pyrénées-Atlantiques (Nouvelle-Aquitaine region) to the east, La Rioja to the south, and the province of Burgos (Castile and León) and Cantabria to the west.
The exact origin of the Basques is disputed; although there are multiple hypotheses in this regard, none has been definitively proven. Basque is an indigenous language in this territory, unrelated to any of its neighboring languages. It is the only language isolate on the European continent and the most widely spoken language isolate in the world.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Euzkadi</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Basque_Country</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Event</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Recreational activities are anything related to play, recreation, leisure, entertainment, and fun. Recreational activities allow, among other things, to spend free time having fun and relaxing, learning through play, recreation, and leisure.
These activities include concerts, technological events, sports, cultural events, culinary events, and many others.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Event</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Event</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#EventType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">This class is divided into the instances defined for this ontology: cultural, technological, gastronomic, sporting, or musical activities or events.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#EventType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">EventType</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Feria_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A fair is an industrial, social, economic, and cultural event—established, temporary, or traveling, periodic or annual—held at a venue and generally encompassing a common theme or purpose.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Feria_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Feria_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Festival_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Festival_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Festival_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A festival, holiday or party is an event generally celebrated by a community and that revolves around some characteristic aspect of that community and its religion or culture.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Festival_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Festival_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Fira_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Barcelona Fair (Catalan: Fira Barcelona) is Barcelona&apos;s largest trade fair institution. Each year, it organizes and hosts more than 270 trade shows, conferences, and corporate events at various intervals. It is therefore the largest and most important trade fair institution in Spain and one of the most important in Europe and the world.
Its annual contribution to the economy of the city and its surroundings is estimated at more than €4.7 billion, creating more than 35,000 jobs, and its activities also generate social and public value.
Each year, 30,000 companies and three million people pass through its facilities.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Fira_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Fira_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#FirstClass</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The First class category is identified as four stars within a five-star system. This classification is defined by the corresponding hotel association, which depends on whether the hotel&apos;s country has adopted this standard system.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#FirstClass</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">FirstClass_Cathegory</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Gastronomic</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A food festival, fair, or gastronomic event is a leisure event whose central theme is food and/or beverages, whether about a particular culinary technique or product, or about the gastronomy of a region, a designation of origin, among others.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Gastronomic</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Gastronomic</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A hotel is a building designed and designed to provide accommodations for people and allow visitors to travel. Hotels provide guests with additional services such as restaurants, swimming pools, and childcare. Hotels are typically categorized according to their level of comfort, location, and level of service.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hotel</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Hotel</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#HotelType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The type of hotel has to do with the category and location of the hotel.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#HotelType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">HotelType</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Delicia_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Our menu offers a wide variety of traditional dishes that are sure to please everyone.
We also have a wide selection of tapas for all tastes: Swiss potatoes, steamed mussels, Galician-style octopus, baby squid, clams, and more.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Delicia_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Restaurant_Delicia_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Delicia_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Restaurant_Delicia_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Palace is a hotel in the Spanish city of Barcelona, ​​formerly known as the Hotel Ritz.
The building, designed by the architect Eduard Ferrès Puig, is located at 668 Gran Vía de las Cortes Catalanas. The former Ritz hotel was founded in 1919, at the initiative of César Ritz. This new establishment was inspired by the Palace and Ritz hotels in Madrid.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Palace_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Petit Palace Arana Bilbao is a modern hotel housed in a stunning 19th-century building, located 200 meters from Bilbao&apos;s Santiago Cathedral.
The elegantly designed rooms at the Arana Bilbao feature air conditioning, a minibar, and a flat-screen TV. Some have a balcony. The private bathroom includes a hydromassage shower. Free Wi-Fi is available.
The Petit Palace Arana Bilbao is opposite the Arriaga Theater and a 20-minute walk from the Guggenheim Museum, which features extraordinary architecture and modern art exhibitions.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Palace_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">It is a good hotel in Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Palace_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Palace_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Royal</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Located on the city&apos;s main commercial and cultural avenue, the birthplace of Catalan modernism, in a unique building with designer rooms, a variety of services, and a great team of professionals for a &quot;royal&quot; stay.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Royal</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Royal_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Royal_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Located in central Zaragoza, 20 minutes from the Feria Congress Center and the high-speed train station, B&amp;B HOTEL Zaragoza Royal offers rooms with free Wi-Fi.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Royal_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Royal_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hostal Sol y K is located in Barcelona, ​​200 metres from Plaza Reial and 400 metres from La Seu, Barcelona Cathedral.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Sol_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">There isn&apos;t actually a Sol hotel in Bilbao.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Sol_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Sol Playa is located on Las Arenas Beach, 100 meters from Valencia&apos;s America&apos;s Cup Port. It offers air-conditioned rooms with satellite TV. Some have sea views.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Sol_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Sol_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Vista</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">I haven&apos;t found any Vista hotels in Barcelona.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_Vista</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_Vista_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_del_Mar</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Located in a privileged location on the shores of the Mediterranean and in the heart of Barcelona, ​​Hotel del Mar is the perfect retreat for an unforgettable vacation.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_del_Mar</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_del_Mar_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_del_Mar_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Housed in a 19th-century building, Hospes Palau de La Mar, Valencia, a member of Design Hotels, offers a stunning lobby, a spa, and a restaurant. Located in Valencia&apos;s Eixample Noble district, it is a 15-minute walk from Valencia&apos;s AVE Train Station.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Hotel_del_Mar_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Hotel_del_Mar_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Italian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Italian gastronomy (cucina italiana) refers to the foods, techniques, dishes, and other culinary traditions of Italy, practiced by its inhabitants and reflecting the cultural richness of its regions and its history.
Italian cuisine is an integral part of Mediterranean cuisines.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Italian</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Italian</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Japanese</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Japan&apos;s national cuisine has evolved over the centuries due to many political, social, and resource changes, beginning in the Ancient Age, when most cuisine was influenced by Chinese culture. Finally, the cuisine changed with the advent of the Middle Ages, which marked the beginning of an abandonment of elitism under the shogunate&apos;s rule.
Japanese cuisine is known for its emphasis on seasonality, quality, and presentation of its ingredients.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Japanese</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Japanese</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Luxury</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">These types of hotels offer a luxurious experience to those who can afford it. Like five-star hotels, the target audience for luxury hotels is the rich and famous, business executives, and others. Luxury hotels and resorts should offer many different types of suites, along with various on-site facilities and services, such as fine dining, fitness centers, spas, and pools.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Luxury</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Luxury</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Luxury_hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Luxury category is identified as five stars within a five-star system. This classification is defined by the corresponding hotel association, which depends on whether the hotel&apos;s country has adopted this standard system.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Luxury_hotel</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">LuxuryHotel_Cathegory</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Madrid</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Madrid is a municipality and a city in Spain. The town, with historical status as a town, is the capital of the State and of the Community of Madrid. In its municipal area, the most populated in Spain, its population is 3,416,771 inhabitants (INE 2024), making it the second most populated city in the European Union, as well as its metropolitan area, with 6,779,888 registered inhabitants.
As the capital of Spain, it houses the headquarters of the Government of the Nation and its ministerial departments, the Cortes Generales (Congress of Deputies and Senate), the Supreme Court and the Constitutional Court, as well as the official residence of the King and Queen of Spain and the President of the Government.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Madrid</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Madrid</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Madrid_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Positive latitudes indicates north of the equator, negative latitudes indicate south. Positive longitudes indicate east of the Greenwich Meridian, negative longitudes indicates west.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Madrid_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Madrid_Coords</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Mediterranean</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Mediterranean diet encompasses the ingredients, preparations, techniques, and other culinary traditions practiced in the Mediterranean region. Despite the enormous diversity of regional characteristics, this region also has many common characteristics, determined by the Mediterranean climate, agriculture, and historical and commercial ties.
The Mediterranean diet is based on grains, legumes, fruits, and vegetables, which is why it is considered essentially (though not exclusively) vegetarian. Legumes are the main source of protein, while olive oil is the main source of fat.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Mediterranean</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Mediterranean</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Metro</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The metro (also called subway, tube, or underground) is an urban railway system located within a city and its metropolitan area. It is characterized as a mass passenger transport system in large cities, connecting various areas and their surrounding areas, with high capacity and frequency, and segregated from other transport systems. Metropolitan railways are built underground or above ground, although most systems use mixed models where sections of both modes are combined. These systems operate different lines that make up a network, stopping at stations that are not far apart and generally located at regular intervals.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Metro</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Company that manages transportation services.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Company that manages transportation services.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Bilbo</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro service in Bilbao.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Bilbo</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro_Bilbo</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Company that manages transportation services.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Metro_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Metro_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Mexican</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Mexican gastronomy is the collection of dishes and culinary techniques from Mexico that are part of the traditions and everyday life of its inhabitants, enriched by contributions from the country&apos;s different regions. This cuisine derives from the experience of the pre-Hispanic region that is now Mexico, the Spanish cuisine of the Iberian Peninsula, and others.
The basis of today&apos;s Mexican cuisine derives largely from the cuisine that existed in pre-Hispanic times, with a predominant use of corn, beans, chili peppers, tomatoes, green tomatoes, squash, avocado, cacao, peanuts, amaranth, vanilla, nopal, agave, cacti, herbs and spices (epazote, hoja santa, papalo, quelites), various birds such as the turkey, and a variety of mammals, fish, and insects. While many ingredients have been adapted to Mexican cuisine through the cultural exchange brought about by the Viceroyalty of New Spain and subsequent centuries, which introduced European, Mediterranean, Asian, and African ingredients such as wheat, rice, coffee, cumin, mint, bay leaf, oregano, parsley, pork, beef, chicken, onion, lemon, orange, plantain, sugarcane, cilantro, cinnamon, cloves, thyme, and pepper, many of which have been widely adopted and even historically cultivated in Mexico, as is the case with coffee and rice.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Mexican</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Mexican</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Monument</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A monument is any work of sufficient value to the human group that erected it. It must be &quot;public and patent.&quot; Although the term initially applied to erected statues, inscriptions, or tombs, its use has since expanded to include any construction with &quot;artistic, archaeological, historical,&quot; or similar value, particularly architectural structures located in an urban area or isolated in a rural setting, which serve as a landmark due to their visibility and become symbols of that place.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Monument</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Monument</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Monument</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
