    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">In Barcelona there are several museums: art (Picasso, MNAC, etc.), history, art, maritime, science, chocolate, and design.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Museo_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The most famous museum in Bilbao is the Guggenheim Museum. It is a contemporary art museum designed by Canadian architect Frank O. Gehry and located in the city of Bilbao (Basque Country), Spain. It is one of the museums affiliated with the Solomon R. Guggenheim Foundation through a franchise or collaboration agreement.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Museo_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Zaragoza Museum is a state-owned museum managed by the Autonomous Community of Aragon. It has been located in the Plaza de los Sitios in the city of Zaragoza, Aragon, since 1908. It is the largest and most representative museum in the entire Aragonese territory. Its collections cover the history of the period from prehistory to the 20th century: Antiquity, the Early Middle Ages, the Renaissance, Gothic, Baroque, Goya and his period, the 19th and 20th centuries. This wide variety is complemented by unique collections of oriental art, ceramics, and ethnology. Its collections of oriental art, such as the Torralba, with over a thousand pieces, and the Pasamar - Onila, with over 300 prints, stand out, making this section of the Museum one of the most important collections of oriental art in Europe.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Museo_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Museo_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Museum</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A museum is a non-profit, permanent institution serving society that researches, collects, preserves, interprets, and exhibits tangible and intangible heritage. Open to the public, accessible, and inclusive, museums foster diversity and sustainability. With the participation of communities, museums operate and communicate ethically and professionally, offering diverse experiences for education, enjoyment, reflection, and knowledge sharing, according to the International Council of Museums (ICOM).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Museum</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Museum</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Museum</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Music</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Music events are activities that involve live music, such as concerts, festivals, and other musical performances. These events can vary in size and format, from small, intimate performances to large festivals with multiple artists and stages.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Music</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Music</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Málaga</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Málaga is a city and municipality in Spain, capital of the province of the same name, located in the autonomous community of Andalusia. With a population of 591,637 as of 1 January 2024, it is the second most populated city in Andalusia and the sixth in Spain, as well as the largest among those that are not regional capitals. It is also the most densely populated urban area in the conurbation formed by the group of towns located along 160 km of the Costa del Sol and the center of a metropolitan area that includes 12 other municipalities with a total of 987,813 registered inhabitants. It is estimated to be the fifth largest metropolitan area in Spain.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Málaga</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Málaga</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Málaga_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Positive latitudes indicates north of the equator, negative latitudes indicate south. Positive longitudes indicate east of the Greenwich Meridian, negative longitudes indicates west.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Málaga_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Málaga_Coords</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Navarra</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Navarre (Basque: Nafarroa), officially the Chartered Community of Navarre (Basque: Nafarroako Foru Komunitatea), is a Spanish chartered community of uniprovincial character located in the north of the Iberian Peninsula. It borders to the northeast with the French region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine (department of Pyrénées-Atlantiques), to the northwest with the Basque Country (provinces of Álava and Guipúzcoa), to the east and southeast with Aragon (provinces of Huesca and Zaragoza) and to the south with La Rioja. The municipality of Petilla de Aragón is an enclave completely surrounded by Zaragoza territory.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Navarra</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Navarra</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#NearBeach</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">&quot;Hotels near the beach&quot; refers to hotels located near a beach. This can mean the hotel is directly across the street from the beach, within walking distance, or even with direct beach access. Proximity to the beach is an important factor for tourists looking for a sun-filled destination, as it allows them to easily enjoy water activities and relax on the sand.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#NearBeach</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">NearBeach</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Chávarri Palace in Bilbao (Vizcaya, Spain) was the headquarters of the Civil Government of Vizcaya since 1943. It is currently the headquarters of the Government Subdelegation in Vizcaya, an institution that replaced the previous Civil Government. It is located in the heart of the Bilbao expansion, with a chamfered main façade facing Plaza Moyúa and a façade line that extends towards Gran Vía and Calle Elcano. It is an eclectic work inspired by Neo-Flemish revivalism, built in 1888 for Víctor Chávarri and his brother Benigno Chávarri by the architect Atanasio de Anduiza[citation needed] according to a design by the Belgian architect Paul Hankar. Some of its rooms were decorated by the painter José Echenagusia Errazquin.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Palacio_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Royal Palace of Valencia (Palau del Real in Valencian), located at 2 General Elio Street in the city of Valencia (Spain), was the former palace of the Kings of Valencia in the &quot;Cap i Casal&quot; (head and common house) of the Kingdom of Valencia, as the city was known. It was also known as the &quot;Palace of the 300 Keys&quot; in allusion to the number of rooms it once had.
From the 11th to the 19th century, it was the royal seat of the kings of the Valencian Taifa, as well as of the monarchs of the Crown of Aragon, the Austrians, and the Bourbons.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Palacio_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Zaaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The palace of the Counts of Sástago is a Renaissance building located in Zaragoza, on Coso Street. It was originally the residence of the Counts of Sástago.
The palace was built between 1570 and 1574. It was commissioned by Artal de Alagón, Count of Sástago and Viceroy of Aragon. In later times, it served as a temporary residence for the monarchs (Philip II and, in 1814, Ferdinand VII) during their stay in Zaragoza.
During the Peninsular War, the building suffered damage caused by a fire at the nearby Convent of San Francisco.
In 1813, the building became the headquarters of the Council of War, presided over by José de Palafox, who resided in the palace until 1815.
Continuing its military ties, the building became the headquarters of the Captaincy General in 1825. And from 1827 to 1847, it served as the headquarters of the army accounting office and the police headquarters. The last Count of Sástago, Joaquín María Fernández de Córdoba, rented the top two floors of the palace to the Zaragoza Casino in 1848. Ricardo Magdalena was the architect in charge of renovating the palace&apos;s halls and casino library.
Since 1981, the building has been owned by the Provincial Council of Zaragoza, which is undergoing extensive restoration work to return it to its original design, which had been significantly altered by the renovations resulting from the palace&apos;s various uses throughout its history. The original 16th-century plans were used for the restoration work.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Palacio_Zaaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Palacio_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Palau_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The National Palace is located on Montjuïc Hill in Barcelona. It was built between 1926 and 1929 for the 1929 Barcelona International Exposition and has housed the National Art Museum of Catalonia since 1934.
It was the main building for the Exposition, designed by architects Eugenio Cendoya and Enric Catà, under the supervision of Pere Domènech i Roura, after the initial project by Josep Puig i Cadafalch and Guillem Busquets was rejected. It has a surface area of ​​32,000 m².  It is in a classicist style inspired by the Spanish Renaissance.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Palau_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Palau_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Pamplona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Pamplona (co-officially in Basque: Iruña) is a Spanish municipality and city, the capital of Navarre.
Pamplona is located in the north of the Iberian Peninsula, in the center of the Pamplona Basin. It extends along both banks of the Arga River and is served by two other rivers, the Elorz (a tributary of the Arga) and the Sadar (a tributary of the Elorz). According to municipal registry data, it has a population of 207,777 (INE 2024), spread over an area of ​​25.9 km². Its metropolitan area totals 334,830 inhabitants spread over an area of ​​488.6 km².</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Pamplona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Pamplona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Pamplona_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Positive latitudes indicates north of the equator, negative latitudes indicate south. Positive longitudes indicate east of the Greenwich Meridian, negative longitudes indicates west.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Pamplona_Coords</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Pamplona_Coords</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Park</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A park (from the French word parc and the English word park) is a natural or semi-natural space that can be located within a city or town and is used as a meadow, garden, or woodland for the recreation and leisure of citizens.
Large areas protected by the state, such as natural and national parks, are dedicated to the protection of wildlife and natural habitats.
Other areas, whether publicly or privately protected, where recreational or exhibition activities are held, such as an amusement park or a zoo, are also known as parks.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Park</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Park</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Park</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Ciutadella Park (Parc de la Ciutadella in Catalan) is located in the Ciutat Vella district of Barcelona, ​​Spain. For many years, it was the only public park in Barcelona. It was laid out on the former grounds of the Ciutadella fortress—hence its name—in the image and likeness of the Luxembourg Gardens in Paris. It was designed by Josep Fontserè and opened in 1881. A few years later, in 1888, it hosted the Barcelona Universal Exposition.
It is located in the La Ribera neighborhood, in the triangle between the Estacion de Francia station, the Arc de Triomf, and the Olympic Village. It is bordered by four main thoroughfares: Passeig de Pujades, Passeig de Picasso, Passeig de Circunvalación, and Carrer de Wellington. It has ten entrances and covers an area of ​​17.42 hectares (31 hectares including the Barcelona Zoo). It is the largest urban park in Barcelona after Montjuic.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Barcelona</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Parque_Barcelona</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Europa Park is a municipal park in the Biscayan city of Bilbao, in the Basque Country (Spain). It is located between the districts of Begoña and Ocharcoaga-Churdínaga, between the neighborhoods of Ocharcoaga, Churdínaga, and Santuchu.
It is a romantic park designed by architect Manuel Salina, featuring paved walkways, green areas, ponds, sculptures, and several buildings, including a greenhouse, a pelota court, a gym, a kiosk, and several pavilions.
The park was restored in 2002 and now includes a dog park, a children&apos;s park, and a sports area.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Bilbao</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Parque_Bilbao</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The Turia Garden is a public urban park located in the old bed of the Turia River in the city of Valencia. It currently occupies approximately 136 hectares (330 acres), which will increase to 160.5 hectares (370 acres) in the future with the completion of the Desembocadura Park. It is approximately 8.5 km (5.2 mi) long and will reach almost 10 km (6.2 mi) with the completion of the last section of the park. It has an average width of approximately 160 m (500 ft). It is one of the largest purely urban gardens in Spain. The park begins at the border with the municipality of Mislata, next to the Canaleta Park in that municipality and the Bioparc Valencia Zoo (inaugurated in 2008), at approximately 19 m (62 ft) above sea level, and ends at Passeig de l&apos;Albereda next to the Oceanogràfic (inaugurated in 2003).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Valencia</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Parque_Valencia</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">José Antonio Labordeta Park is a park in the city of Zaragoza, Spain, inaugurated in 1929 and located in the University district. From its inauguration and for 81 years, it was officially called Primo de Rivera Park and popularly known as the Grande Park. However, after the death of singer-songwriter and politician José Antonio Labordeta in 2010, the Zaragoza City Council named the park after him.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Parque_Zaragoza</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Parque_Zaragoza</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Public_Bycicle</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The bicycle is a human-powered personal transport vehicle. It is propelled by the traveler himself, who operates the vehicle with the muscular effort of the legs, in particular by means of pedals or cranks. [2] Its basic components are two wheels, [2] generally of equal diameter and arranged in line, a pedal transmission system, a frame that gives it the structure and integrates the components, a handlebar to control the direction and a saddle to sit on. One-way movement is obtained by turning the symmetrically arranged pedals with the legs, each with a lever known as a crank are assembled in the frame, and in general the right pedal is the one with the largest star or plate where the chain is mounted, which in turn turns the sprocket, which finally turns the rear wheel on the ground, then causing the displacement.
Public bicycle rental systems have also been available for several years. They are different from conventional rentals because the stations form a network throughout the city, enabling one-way transportation to work, school, or shopping. This makes bicycles accessible to everyone and reduces the problem of private bicycle theft. Users must provide electronic identification before picking up a bicycle to prevent theft and vandalism.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Public_Bycicle</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Public_BiCycle</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Region</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The term region can refer to a &quot;portion of territory&quot; with certain common characteristics, such as climate, topography, or administration. It can also refer to the &quot;geographic, historical, cultural, and social characteristics&quot; of a nation.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Region</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Region</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Region</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Region</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:sameAs"/>
        <IRI>#Region</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Region</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
