        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#TransportService</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A shuttle service is a type of transportation operated by a company in a specific city. In a city, there may be more than one company operating the same type of transportation.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#TransportService</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">TransportService</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#TransportType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Public transport is the term applied to collective passenger transportation. Unlike private transportation, public transportation users must adapt to the schedules and routes offered by the operator and depend to a greater or lesser extent on government regulatory intervention.
Travelers usually share the means of transportation, and the different units are available to the general public. This includes various modes of transportation, such as buses, taxis, trolleybuses, trams, trains, suburban railways, ferries, cable cars, funiculars, and bicycles.
In our case, we differentiate between public bicycles, underground, buses, taxis, and trams.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#TransportType</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">TransportType</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>#Transportation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Transport (in British English) or transportation (in American English) is the intentional movement of humans, animals, and goods from one location to another. Modes of transport include air, land (rail and road), water, cable, pipelines, and space. The field can be divided into infrastructure, vehicles, and operations. Transport enables human trade, which is essential for the development of civilizations.
Transport infrastructure consists of both fixed facilities, including roads, railways, airways, waterways, canals, and pipelines, and terminals such as airports, railway stations, bus stations, warehouses, trucking terminals, refueling depots (including fuel docks and fuel stations), and seaports.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:isDefinedBy"/>
        <IRI>#Transportation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:label"/>
        <IRI>#Transportation</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Transportation</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
